ient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a PD subtyping study using multimodal data and ML/DL, with no natural-language text component, no text-bio bridge, and no generative model; thus it does not meet inclusion criteria.",No text/language component described; the study uses clinical progression data and multi-omics but no natural-language text or text-bio alignment.,"The abstract describes subtyping Parkinson's disease using machine learning and deep learning for phenotypic progression trajectories, with no mention of generative architectures, text generation, or biological sequence generation. No foundation-model characteristics are present.","The abstract describes a PD subtyping study using multimodal data and ML/DL, with no natural-language text component, no text-bio bridge, and no generative model; thus it does not meet inclusion criteria.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes multimodal clinical, imaging, and omics data analysis with machine learning but provides no mention of natural-language text, a text‑bio alignment, or any generative mechanism. Therefore it lacks a text component, bridge, and generative model, leading to exclusion under EC3_not_generative.","The study applies machine‑learning/deep‑learning to multimodal clinical, imaging, genetic and transcriptomic data without introducing a new model. No natural‑language text, prompts, or documents are described, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge.","The abstract focuses on PD subtyping and drug repurposing using machine learning and deep learning, without describing a generative architecture or foundation-model pretraining.","The abstract describes multimodal clinical, imaging, and omics data analysis with machine learning but provides no mention of natural-language text, a text‑bio alignment, or any generative mechanism. Therefore it lacks a text component, bridge, and generative model, leading to exclusion under EC3_not_generative.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The study uses machine learning on multimodal biological data (clinical, CSF, imaging, genetics) without presenting a generative text‑bio model or any natural‑language component. | The abstract describes PD subtyping and drug repurposing using multimodal data and existing ML/DL methods, with no mention of a generative architecture.","The study uses machine learning on multimodal biological data (clinical, CSF, imaging, genetics) without presenting a generative text‑bio model or any natural‑language component.","The abstract describes PD subtyping and drug repurposing using multimodal data and existing ML/DL methods, with no mention of a generative architecture.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:148:331,Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,"LLM embeddings used as prior, no explicit text-bio alignment","GRN inference, no generative mechanism described",wrapper_boundary,"GRNPT uses frozen LLM embeddings as prior knowledge for gene regulatory network inference from scRNA-seq data, making it an application_wrapper. No generative or explicit text-bio conditioning is present.","GRNPT uses LLM embeddings as side features for GRN inference from scRNA-seq, not as a generative text-bio model or substantive text component; therefore it is an application wrapper.","The abstract describes a Transformer-based framework for gene regulatory network inference, a predictive task, with no mention of generative mechanisms such as autoregressive, decoder, or sequence generation. The use of LLM embeddings does not make the candidate model generative.","GRNPT uses frozen LLM embeddings as prior knowledge for gene regulatory network inference from scRNA-seq data, making it an application_wrapper. No generative or explicit text-bio conditioning is present.",Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,unclear,none,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"GRNPT uses frozen LLM embeddings as a prior for a downstream GRN inference predictor, with no natural-language text input/output or generative mechanism, making it an application_wrapper.","GRNPT uses external LLM embeddings as features for a downstream GRN inference task, which is an application wrapper; no substantive text component or text-bio bridge in the candidate model itself.","The abstract describes GRNPT as a Transformer-based framework for gene regulatory network inference (predicting regulatory relationships), not a generative model. No generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion is mentioned.","GRNPT uses frozen LLM embeddings as a prior for a downstream GRN inference predictor, with no natural-language text input/output or generative mechanism, making it an application_wrapper.",Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,none,GRNPT ... to capture regulatory patterns ... inference,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes GRNPT as a framework that integrates LLM embeddings for gene regulatory network inference, not a generative model. No natural-language text input/output or text-bio alignment is present; the LLM embeddings are used as prior knowledge for a downstream prediction task, making it an application_wrapper.","GRNPT uses LLM embeddings as side features for GRN inference, not as a generative text-bio model; it is an application wrapper around an existing LLM.","The paper describes GRNPT, a framework for gene regulatory network inference using LLM embeddings and a TCN autoencoder, but it is a predictive/classification task (inferring regulatory relationships) with no generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive generation, or diffusion.","The abstract describes GRNPT as a framework that integrates LLM embeddings for gene regulatory network inference, not a generative model. No natural-language text input/output or text-bio alignment is present; the LLM embeddings are used as prior knowledge for a downstream prediction task, making it an application_wrapper.",Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a novel transformer