es tumor-type classification and localization from genomic data using a large language model; this is a predictive classification task with no generative mechanism mentioned.,,Large language models enable tumor-type classification and localization of cancers of unknown primary from genomic data,"while DNA methylation and transcriptomic profiling classify 75%  Second, expanding the  feature space to include multimodal data To enable training of the large language model (LLM),",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,unclear,genomic data,none,tumor-type classification,clear_exclude,"The abstract only references genomic data and tumor-type classification, with no mention of natural-language text or generation. This indicates the LLM is used as a wrapper for a discriminative task, not as a new multimodal generative model.","The title describes a new LLM‑based approach for tumor‑type classification from genomic data, indicating a primary model paper with a biological modality, but the abstract lacks explicit mention of natural‑language text or a text‑bio mapping.","The title/abstract describe using a large language model for tumor-type classification and localization, with no mention of a generative mechanism; the work appears to apply an existing LLM to a downstream task.","The abstract only references genomic data and tumor-type classification, with no mention of natural-language text or generation. This indicates the LLM is used as a wrapper for a discriminative task, not as a new multimodal generative model.",Large language models enable tumor-type classification and localization of cancers of unknown primary from genomic data,"while DNA methylation and transcriptomic profiling classify 75%  Second, expanding the  feature space to include multimodal data To enable training of the large language model (LLM),",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,text_component_unclear,application_wrapper,unclear,unclear,no,unclear,,,,,"The abstract reports using a large language model to classify tumor types from genomic data, confirming a biological modality, but provides no clear indication of natural-language text input or a substantive text-bio bridge, and the work appears to apply an existing LLM rather than present a new primary model. | The abstract focuses on tumor-type classification with a large language model and does not describe any generative mechanism; the work appears to be an application of an existing LLM rather than a primary generative model.","The abstract reports using a large language model to classify tumor types from genomic data, confirming a biological modality, but provides no clear indication of natural-language text input or a substantive text-bio bridge, and the work appears to apply an existing LLM rather than present a new primary model.",The abstract focuses on tumor-type classification with a large language model and does not describe any generative mechanism; the work appears to be an application of an existing LLM rather than a primary generative model.,
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:153:69,Multi-modal integration of histopathology and transcriptomics reveals STAB1,"Identification of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) as a central immunosuppressive subpopulation enriched in apoptotic tumor regions and further expanded following neoadjuvant therapy. Demonstration that STAB1 drives M2 macrophage polarization and efferocytosis through activation of the mTORC1 signaling pathway. Revealing a complex cell–cell communication network linking STAB1^+ TAMs, neutrophils, and malignant epithelial cells, forming a positive feedback loop that reinforces immune evasion. Development of a clinically applicable, deep learning–based prognostic model, which translates histopathological features into quantifiable risk indices and demonstrates predictive power comparable to that of the TNM staging system. Background Colon adenocarcinoma (COAD) has a limited response to immunotherapy due to its immunologically “cold” tumor microenvironment (TME). Efferocytosis is an important process that regulates the TME, but its mechanism and clinical significance in COAD are unclear. Methods We integrated histopathological images, transcriptomic profiles, and clinical data from 387 COAD patients. Image features were extracted using ResNet50 and CellProfiler, followed by construction of a multimodal machine learning model to evaluate prognostic risk. We further combined bulk RNA-seq, single-cell RNA-seq, and spatial transcriptomics to comprehensively characterize efferocytosis-associated immune cell subsets and signaling pathways. Results The efferocytosis-based risk model demonstrated strong prognostic performance across multiple time points and remained independent of conventional clinical indicators. Mechanistically, we identified a subset of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) enriched in COAD tumors, exhibiting enhanced efferocytosis activity, M2-like polarization, and mTORC1 signaling activation. In vitro, STAB1 expression was essential for IL-4-induced M2 polarization, and its inhibition attenuated the formation of immunosuppressive TAMs. Single-cell and spatial transcriptomic analyses revealed that this macrophage population was transcriptionally distinct and increased in abundance following neoadjuvant therapy. Conclusion This study establishes a multimodal prognostic system that integrates histopathological imaging with molecular profiling, and for the first time reveals the pivotal role of STAB1^+ TAMs in orchestrating the immunosuppressive TME via efferocytosis and mTORC1 activation. Our findings provide both a clinically applicable risk assessment tool and a potential therapeutic target. Targeting STAB1 may broaden the benefit of immunotherapy for COAD patients with limited responses to immune checkpoint blockade. Graphical Abstract",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Multi-modal integration of histopathology and transcriptomics reveals STAB1,"Identification of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) as a central immunosuppressive subpopulation enriched in apoptotic tumor regions and further expanded following neoadjuvant therapy. Demonstration that STAB1 drives M2 macrophage polarization and efferocytosis through activation of the mTORC1 signaling pathway. Revealing a complex cell–cell communication network linking STAB1^+ TAMs, neutrophils, and malignant epithelial cells, forming a positive feedback loop that reinforces immune evasion. Development of a clinically applicable, deep learning–based prognostic model, which translates histopathological features into quantifiable risk indices and demonstrates predictive power comparable to that of the TNM staging system. Background Colon adenocarcinoma (COAD) has a limited response to immunotherapy due to its immunologically “cold” tumor microenvironment (TME). Efferocytosis is an important process that regulates the TME, but its mechanism and clinical significance in COAD are unclear. Methods We integrated histopathological images, transcriptomic profiles, and clinical data from 387 COAD patients. Image features were extracted using ResNet50 and CellProfiler, followed by construction of a multimodal machine learning model to evaluate prognostic risk. We further combined bulk RNA-seq, single-cell RNA-seq, and spatial transcriptomics to comprehensively characterize efferocytosis-associated immune cell subsets and signaling pathways. Results The efferocytosis-based risk model demonstrated strong prognostic performance across multiple time points and remained independent of conventional clinical indicators. Mechanistically, we identified a subset of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) enriched in COAD tumors, exhibiting enhanced efferocytosis activity, M2-like polarization, and mTORC1 signaling activation. In vitro, STAB1 expression was essential for IL-4-induced M2 polarization, and its inhibition attenuated the formation of immunosuppressive TAMs. Single-cell and spatial transcriptomic analyses revealed that this macrophage population was transcriptionally distinct and increased in abundance following neoadjuvant therapy. Conclusion This study establishes a multimodal prognostic system that integrates histopathological imaging with molecular profiling, and for the first time reveals the pivotal role of STAB1^+ TAMs in orchestrating the immunosuppressive TME via efferocytosis and mTORC1 activation. Our findings provide both a clinically applicable risk assessment tool and a potential therapeutic target. Targeting STAB1 may broaden the benefit of immunotherapy for COAD patients with limited responses to immune checkpoint blockade. Graphical Abstract",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"Abstract presents a prognostic model integrating histopathology and transcriptomics; no natural-language text or generative mechanism is described, so it fails EC3 (not generative) and EC2 (no text component).","The abstract describes a multimodal prognostic model integrating histopathology and transcriptomics, but lacks any text/language component or generative text-bio modeling; therefore it does not meet the criteria for a generative text-bio foundational model.","The abstract describes a prognostic risk model based on histopathology and transcriptomics; it uses deep learning for prediction, not generation, and lacks any generative mechanism or foundation-model characteristics.","Abstract presents a prognostic model integrating histopathology and transcriptomics; no natural-language text or generative mechanism is described, so it fails EC3 (not generative) and EC2 (no text component).",Multi-modal integration of histopathology and transcriptomics reveals STAB1,"Identification of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) as a central immunosuppressive subpopulation enriched in apoptotic tumor regions and further expanded following neoadjuvant therapy. Demonstration that STAB1 drives M2 macrophage polarization and efferocytosis through activation of the mTORC1 signaling pathway. Revealing a complex cell–cell communication network linking STAB1^+ TAMs, neutrophils, and malignant epithelial cells, forming a positive feedback loop that reinforces immune evasion. Development of a clinically applicable, deep learning–based prognostic model, which translates histopathological features into quantifiable risk indices and demonstrates predictive power comparable to that of the TNM staging system. Background Colon adenocarcinoma (COAD) has a limited response to immunotherapy due to its immunologically “cold” tumor microenvironment (TME). Efferocytosis is an important process that regulates the TME, but its mechanism and clinical significance in COAD are unclear. Methods We integrated histopathological images, transcriptomic profiles, and clinical data from 387 COAD patients. Image features were extracted using ResNet50 and CellProfiler, followed by construction of a multimodal machine learning model to evaluate prognostic risk. We further combined bulk RNA-seq, single-cell RNA-seq, and spatial transcriptomics to comprehensively characterize efferocytosis-associated immune cell subsets and signaling pathways. Results The efferocytosis-based risk model demonstrated strong prognostic performance across multiple time points and remained independent of conventional clinical indicators. Mechanistically, we identified a subset of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) enriched in COAD tumors, exhibiting enhanced efferocytosis activity, M2-like polarization, and mTORC1 signaling activation. In vitro, STAB1 expression was essential for IL-4-induced M2 polarization, and its inhibition attenuated the formation of immunosuppressive TAMs. Single-cell and spatial transcriptomic analyses revealed that this macrophage population was transcriptionally distinct and increased in abundance following neoadjuvant therapy. Conclusion This study establishes a multimodal prognostic system that integrates histopathological imaging with molecular profiling, and for the first time reveals the pivotal role of STAB1^+ TAMs in orchestrating the immunosuppressive TME via efferocytosis and mTORC1 activation. Our findings provide both a clinically applicable risk assessment tool and a potential therapeutic target. Targeting STAB1 may broaden the benefit of immunotherapy for COAD patients with limited responses to immune checkpoint blockade. Graphical Abstract",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"No text/language component; the model integrates histopathology and transcriptomics for prognostic risk prediction, not a generative text-bio model. | The paper develops a deep learning prognostic model for risk prediction using histopathological features; no generative mechanism (decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, etc.) is mentioned, and the model is not a foundation model.","No text/language component; the model integrates histopathology and transcriptomics for prognostic risk prediction, not a generative text-bio model.","The paper develops a deep learning prognostic model for risk prediction using histopathological features; no generative mechanism (decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, etc.) is mentioned, and the model is not a foundation model.",,Multi-modal integration of histopathology and transcriptomics reveals STAB1,"Identification of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) as a central immunosuppressive subpopulation enriched in apoptotic tumor regions and further expanded following neoadjuvant therapy. Demonstration that STAB1 drives M2 macrophage polarization and efferocytosis through activation of the mTORC1 signaling pathway. Revealing a complex cell–cell communication network linking STAB1^+ TAMs, neutrophils, and malignant epithelial cells, forming a positive feedback loop that reinforces immune evasion. Development of a clinically applicable, deep learning–based prognostic model, which translates histopathological features into quantifiable risk indices and demonstrates predictive power comparable to that of the TNM staging system. Background Colon adenocarcinoma (COAD) has a limited response to immunotherapy due to its immunologically “cold” tumor microenvironment (TME). Efferocytosis is an important process that regulates the TME, but its mechanism and clinical significance in COAD are unclear. Methods We integrated histopathological images, transcriptomic profiles, and clinical data from 387 COAD patients. Image features were extracted using ResNet50 and CellProfiler, followed by construction of a multimodal machine learning model to evaluate prognostic risk. We further combined bulk RNA-seq, single-cell RNA-seq, and spatial transcriptomics to comprehensively characterize efferocytosis-associated immune cell subsets and signaling pathways. Results The efferocytosis-based risk model demonstrated strong prognostic performance across multiple time points and remained independent of conventional clinical indicators. Mechanistically, we identified a subset of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) enriched in COAD tumors, exhibiting enhanced efferocytosis activity, M2-like polarization, and mTORC1 signaling activation. In vitro, STAB1 expression was essential for IL-4-induced M2 polarization, and its inhibition attenuated the formation of immunosuppressive TAMs. Single-cell and spatial transcriptomic analyses revealed that this macrophage population was transcriptionally distinct and increased in abundance following neoadjuvant therapy. Conclusion This study establishes a multimodal prognostic system that integrates histopathological imaging with molecular profiling, and for the first time reveals the pivotal role of STAB1^+ TAMs in orchestrating the immunosuppressive TME via efferocytosis and mTORC1 activation. Our findings provide both a clinically applicable risk assessment tool and a potential therapeutic target. Targeting STAB1 may broaden the benefit of immunotherapy for COAD patients with limited responses to immune checkpoint blockade. Graphical Abstract",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a prognostic risk model integrating histopathology and transcriptomics using ResNet50 and machine learning, with no text component, no text-bio bridge, and no generative mechanism; it is an application wrapper, not a generative text-bio foundational model.","The abstract describes a prognostic model integrating histopathology and transcriptomics, with no mention of any text/language component or natural language processing. Therefore, it does not satisfy the text component criterion.","The abstract describes a prognostic risk model using deep learning on histopathology and transcriptomics, with no mention of generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive, or sequence generation."