./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:196:2191,BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,"biological text reasoning, LLMs, cross-modal question answering, interactive dialogue",aligns them with LLMs through projection layers; unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs,"enables richer, generative outputs; cross-modal question answering and interactive dialogue",clear_include,"Scope reviewer correctly identifies a primary model paper with explicit text-bio alignment and generative outputs; architecture reviewer's application_wrapper label is not supported because the abstract presents a new multimodal alignment and instruction-tuning framework that generates natural-language outputs, not a downstream classifier using frozen LLM embeddings.","Presents BioVERSE, a two-stage approach aligning biomedical modalities to LLMs via projection layers and instruction tuning, enabling cross-modal reasoning and generative outputs. Clearly includes text component (LLM, instruction tuning, dialogue) and text-bio bridge (alignment of bio modalities to LLM space).","The abstract describes adapting pretrained BioFMs and aligning them with existing LLMs via projection layers and instruction tuning, rather than presenting a new generative architecture; the generative outputs originate from the external LLM.","Scope reviewer correctly identifies a primary model paper with explicit text-bio alignment and generative outputs; architecture reviewer's application_wrapper label is not supported because the abstract presents a new multimodal alignment and instruction-tuning framework that generates natural-language outputs, not a downstream classifier using frozen LLM embeddings.",BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,"LLMs, biological text reasoning, cross-modal question answering, interactive dialogue","aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers; unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs","enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs; cross-modal question answering, interactive, explainable dialogue",clear_include,"The abstract presents a new multi-modal alignment framework (BioVERSE) that adapts pretrained BioFMs and aligns them with LLMs for generative reasoning tasks, meeting all inclusion criteria; the architecture reviewer's wrapper classification is overruled because the approach is central and not merely using a frozen LLM for a downstream classifier.","The abstract presents BIOVERSE, a new model that aligns biomedical modalities with LLMs for cross-modal reasoning, including text-based question answering and dialogue, satisfying all criteria.","BioVERSE adapts pretrained BioFMs and aligns them with LLMs via projection layers and instruction tuning; it does not propose a new generative architecture or foundation model, making it an application wrapper.","The abstract presents a new multi-modal alignment framework (BioVERSE) that adapts pretrained BioFMs and aligns them with LLMs for generative reasoning tasks, meeting all inclusion criteria; the architecture reviewer's wrapper classification is overruled because the approach is central and not merely using a frozen LLM for a downstream classifier.",BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,"cross-modal question answering, interactive explainable dialogue",unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs,"enables richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs",clear_include,"Abstract presents a new model (BIOVERSE) that aligns biomedical modalities with LLMs through projection layers and instruction tuning, enabling generative tasks like dialogue and question answering. This is a primary model paper, not a wrapper, and the generative and text-bridge criteria are clearly supported by the evidence.","BIOVERSE is a primary model that aligns biomedical modalities to LLMs via projection layers and instruction tuning, enabling cross-modal reasoning and generative outputs, satisfying all criteria.","The paper describes an alignment method that adapts pretrained BioFMs and LLMs, not a new generative architecture; it is an application wrapper around existing models.","Abstract presents a new model (BIOVERSE) that aligns biomedical modalities with LLMs through projection layers and instruction tuning, enabling generative tasks like dialogue and question answering. This is a primary model paper, not a wrapper, and the generative and text-bridge criteria are clearly supported by the evidence.",BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces BIOVERSE as a new multimodal model that aligns biomedical data modalities with LLMs, includes natural‑language dialogue, and explicitly describes a bridge between modalities and language. | The abstract introduces BIOVERSE, a new two‑stage architecture that aligns pretrained BioFMs with LLMs and applies instruction tuning, yielding generative outputs such as dialogue, which demonstrates a generative model; it also leverages large pretrained models, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.","The abstract introduces BIOVERSE as a new multimodal model that aligns biomedical data modalities with LLMs, includes natural‑language dialogue, and explicitly describes a bridge between modalities and language.","The abstract introduces BIOVERSE, a new two‑stage architecture that aligns pretrained BioFMs with LLMs and applies instruction tuning, yielding generative outputs such as dialogue, which demonstrates a generative model; it also leverages large pretrained models, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",,BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces BIOVERSE as a new multimodal model that aligns biomedical modalities to LLMs, providing biological data handling, natural‑language components, and a clear text‑bio bridge for reasoning. | The abstract presents BIOVERSE, a novel two‑stage architecture that aligns pretrained biomedical encoders with LLMs and explicitly mentions ""enabling richer, generative outputs,"" indicating a generative foundation‑model contribution.","The abstract introduces BIOVERSE as a new multimodal model that aligns biomedical modalities to LLMs, providing biological data handling, natural‑language components, and a clear text‑bio bridge for reasoning.","The abstract presents BIOVERSE, a novel two‑stage architecture that aligns pretrained biomedical encoders with LLMs and explicitly mentions ""enabling richer, generative outputs,"" indicating a generative foundation‑model contribution.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:199:                Cell type annotation is an essential step in single-cell RNA-sequencing analysis, and numerous annotation methods are available. Most require a combination of computational and domain-specific expertise, and they frequently yield inconsistent results that can be challenging to interpret. Large language models have the potential to expand accessibility while reducing manual input and improving accuracy, but existing approaches suffer from hyperconfidence, hallucinations, and lack of reasoning. To address these limitations, we developed CASSIA for automated, accurate, and interpretable cell annotation of single-cell RNA-sequencing data. As demonstrated in analyses of over 970 cell types, CASSIA improves annotation accuracy in benchmark datasets as well as complex and rare cell populations, and also provides users with reasoning and quality assessment to ensure interpretability, guard against hallucinations, and calibrate confidence.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"CASSIA uses a multi-agent LLM for cell annotation (classification), not generation; no natural-language text component or text-bio alignment is described, making it an application wrapper.","The abstract presents CASSIA as a multi-agent LLM for automated cell annotation of single-cell RNA-seq data, which uses LLMs as reasoning agents for a downstream classification task. No natural-language text input, output, or text-bio generation is described, so it qualifies as an application wrapper.","The abstract describes CASSIA as a multi-agent LLM for cell annotation, providing reasoning and quality assessment, indicating generative text output, but does not specify whether it is a new foundation model or a wrapper, nor provide evidence of broad pretraining or generative architecture details.","CASSIA uses a multi-agent LLM for cell annotation (classification), not generation; no natural-language text component or text-bio alignment is described, making it an application wrapper.","CASSIA: a multi-agent large language model for reference free, interpretable, and automated cell annotation of single-cell RNA-sequencing data","Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:200:                Cell type annotation is an essential step in single-cell RNA-sequencing analysis, and numerous annotation methods are available. Most require a combination of computational and domain-specific expertise, and they frequently yield inconsistent results that can be challenging to interpret. Large language models have the potential to expand accessibility while reducing manual input and improving accuracy, but existing approaches suffer from hyperconfidence, hallucinations, and lack of reasoning. To address these limitations, we developed CASSIA for automated, accurate, and interpretable cell annotation of single-cell RNA-sequencing data. As demonstrated in analyses of over 970 cell types, CASSIA improves annotation accuracy in benchmark datasets as well as complex and rare cell populations, and also provides users with reasoning and quality assessment to ensure interpretability, guard against hallucinations, and calibrate confidence.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,unclear,none,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract presents CASSIA as a multi-agent LLM for cell annotation, but does not describe natural-language text input/output or a generative mechanism; it is an application wrapper using an LLM for classification, consistent with the architecture reviewer's assessment.","CASSIA is a new multi-agent LLM that generates natural-language reasoning from scRNA-seq data for cell annotation, making the text component and text-bio bridge central to the model.","CASSIAimary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a cross-modality predictive model that infers risk scores from histopathology images. It does not describe a generative architecture or foundation-model-style pretraining.","evidence_snippet":"\"deep learning models were then trained to infer these biomarkers directly from WSI\" and \"stratify metastatic outcomes\""},{"record_id":"rec_000178","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review of AI-enabled text mining, not an original model paper. It surveys transformers and LLMs rather than presenting a candidate generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review examines the evolution of AI-driven text mining\" and \"Literature encompassing natural language processing (NLP), transformer-based models\""},{"record_id":"rec_000179","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The study uses radiomic features, single-cell transcriptomics, and drug screening to stratify prognosis and suggest therapies. The abstract does not describe a generative model or foundation-model-like pretraining.","evidence_snippet":"\"identify prognostic RFs\" and \"Computational drug screening\""},{"record_id":"rec_000180","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a narrative review/series article on AI in colorectal surgery rather than an original model report. It mentions foundation models only in passing without describing a candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This is Part II of a two-part series\" and \"Comprehensive literature review\""},{"record_id":"rec_000181","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_r