on_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000322","title":"Frontier-eng: Benchmarking self-evolving agents on real-world engineering tasks with generative optimization","abstract":"circuits, and the OpenProblems single-cell analysis platform; (3)  predict_modality predicts  cross-modality gene expression,  For gpt-5.4, we replace the original run with the full 47-task","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000322","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title frames this as benchmarking, and the abstract snippet mentions a platform and cross-modality gene expression rather than a candidate text-bio model. The bio terms are present, but the record reads as a benchmark/resource rather than a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"Benchmarking self-evolving agents\"; \"OpenProblems single-cell analysis platform\"; \"predict_modality predicts cross-modality gene expression\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000322","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title frames this as benchmarking, and the abstract fragment mentions a platform and tasks rather than a clearly described candidate model. The generative wording is present, but the record reads as a benchmark/resource.","evidence_snippet":"\"Benchmarking self-evolving agents\"; \"generative optimization\"; \"OpenProblems single-cell analysis platform\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000324","title":"GeneVAR: Causal MeanFlow for Autoregressive Gene-to-WSI Tile Synthesis","abstract":"under continuous transcriptomic conditioning, akin to GPT-2.  with gene expression  profile-infused deep generative models Causal disentanglement for single-cell representations","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Proceedings of the …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000324","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract describe a generative model for gene-to-WSI tile synthesis with transcriptomic conditioning. The abstract does not clearly support a substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"Autoregressive Gene-to-WSI Tile Synthesis\"; \"continuous transcriptomic conditioning\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000324","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract describe an autoregressive synthesis model with transcriptomic conditioning, which is clearly generative. The abstract does not clearly show broad pretraining or other foundation-model-like characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"\"Autoregressive Gene-to-WSI Tile Synthesis\"; \"continuous transcriptomic conditioning\"; \"akin to GPT-2\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000325","title":"Systematic analysis of programmed cell death–related genes in sepsis reveals immune heterogeneity and enables patient stratification","abstract":"Background\n                    Sepsis is a life-threatening syndrome characterized by dysregulated host response to infection. Programmed cell death (PCD) pathways, including apoptosis, necroptosis, and pyroptosis, play a critical role in sepsis pathophysiology. However, the crosstalk between PCD, metabolic reprogramming, and immune heterogeneity remains poorly understood.\n                  \n                  \n                    Methods\n                    \n                      We analyzed transcriptomic data from the GSE57065 and GSE95233 datasets. Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) were intersected with programmed cell death-related genes (PCDRGs). Consensus clustering was performed to identify molecular subtypes. A risk stratification model was constructed using LASSO and Random Forest algorithms. The immune landscape was deconvoluted using CIBERSORT. To validate cellular mechanisms, we integrated single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data, analyzing cell type-specific expression, intercellular communication (CellChat), and pathway enrichment. Additionally, the reliability of the identified signature was verified in a murine sepsis model (burn injury combined with\n                      Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n                      infection) using quantitative real-time PCR (qPCR).\n                    \n                  \n                  \n                    Results\n                    We identified 14 dysregulated PCDRGs in sepsis. Clustering based on these genes revealed two distinct patient subgroups: an “adaptive-dominant” cluster (enriched in T cell activation) and an “innate-dominant” cluster (enriched in neutrophil degranulation and heme metabolism). A 5-gene risk signature (IFNGR1, PYGL, JAK2, HMGB2, BMX) was constructed, which robustly stratified patients in the discovery and external validation cohorts (AUC &amp;gt; 0.97). Experimental validation in septic mice confirmed that the expression levels of all five hub genes were significantly upregulated in peripheral blood, consistent with the human transcriptomic findings. High-risk patients exhibited severe immune imbalance characterized by neutrophil/monocyte expansion and lymphocyte depletion. Single-cell analysis confirmed that hub genes were predominantly expressed in myeloid lineages. Furthermore, high-risk patients displayed intensified Monocyte-Dendritic Cell (DC) crosstalk driven by inflammatory signaling, with transcriptional programs in these cells converging on neutrophil degranulation pathways.\n                  \n                  \n                    Conclusion\n                    This study establishes a robust PCDRG-based risk signature that captures the immune–metabolic heterogeneity of sepsis. The signature enables patient stratification and reflects disease-associated immune states. Our findings suggest a potential association between dysregulated PCD genes in myeloid cells and an innate immune–dominant, hyper-inflammatory phenotype, providing potential biomarkers and therapeutic insights for sepsis.","doi":"10.3389/fcell.2026.1805864","year":"2026","venue":"Frontiers in Cell and …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000325","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This is a standard sepsis transcriptomics/risk-signature study with clustering, LASSO, Random Forest, and scRNA-seq validation, not a generative text-bio model. It clearly has biological data but no text component.","evidence_snippet":"\"We analyzed transcriptomic data\"; \"single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000325","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a transcriptomics/risk-signature study using clustering, LASSO, Random Forest, and scRNA-seq validation. It does not describe a genuinely generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"We analyzed transcriptomic data\"; \"A risk stratification model was constructed using LASSO and Random Forest algorithms\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000327","title":"Target identification and assessment in the era of AI","abstract":"single-cell transcriptomes, used a generative approach with normalized (non-ranked) gene  expression  Its pretraining adheres to the standard GPT framework but leverages a domain-","doi":"10.1038/s41573-026-01412-8","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Reviews Drug …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000327","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title and venue suggest a review-style article, while the abstract snippet mentions a specific generative approach and GPT framework. The record is ambiguous, but it does not clearly establish a primary text-bio model paper.","evidence_snippet":"title \"Target identification and assessment in the era of AI\"; abstract snippet mentions \"pretraining adheres to the standard GPT framework\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000327","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title and venue suggest a review article, but the abstract fragment mentions a GPT-like generative approach. There is not enough context to treat this as a primary model paper or to verify foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"title \"Target identification and assessment in the era of AI\"; abstract fragment \"Its pretraining adheres to the standard GPT framework\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000330","title":"TMEM92 drives EMT-associated invasiveness, cisplatin resistance, and immune suppression in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","abstract":"findings at the single-cell level, single-cell RNA-sequencing  generative AI and AI-assisted  technologies in the writing process During the preparation of this work, the authors used GPT-","doi":"10.1038/s41598-026-49694-7","year":"2026","venue":"Scientific Reports","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000330","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper is a cancer biology study, and the only GPT mention is in the writing process. That is side-use of an external LLM, not a substantive text-bio model.","evidence_