ecord_id":"rec_000336","title":"AI-Driven Predictive Modeling for Accelerated Development of Cell and Gene Therapy Products","abstract":"Generative AI models, including generative adversarial  , and single-cell atlases that provide  reference gene expression  CRISPR-GPT functions as an interactive assistant that predicts","doi":"","year":"NA","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000336","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract reads like a broad discussion of generative AI applications in cell and gene therapy, including CRISPR-GPT as an example. It does not present a primary text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"Generative AI models ... CRISPR-GPT functions as an interactive assistant"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000336","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a broad discussion of generative AI applications and an example assistant, not a primary model description. It does not clearly present a specific generative text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"Generative AI models ... CRISPR-GPT functions as an interactive assistant"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000337","title":"CARIBOU: Computational AI Research Interface for Bioinformatics, Omics, and Unifying Agents","abstract":"SUMMARY\n                The growing gap between biological data generation and the availability of expert analysts motivates the development of AI systems capable of autonomously performing meaningful computational biology workflows. Here, we present CARIBOU (Computational AI Research Interface for Bioinformatics, Omics, and Unifying Agents), a multi-agent framework designed for practical deployment within institutional research computing environments. CARIBOU organizes specialized AI agents through researcher-modifiable blueprints that encode analytical roles, domain knowledge, and workflow guidance. All analyses are executed within reproducible computational environments compatible with Singularity/Apptainer-based high-performance computing (HPC) systems commonly used in academic research, while maintaining a persistent shared analytical state across multiple stages of analysis. This design enables CARIBOU to iteratively execute, troubleshoot, and refine bioinformatics workflows rather than simply generate static code. We evaluate CARIBOU across unit-task benchmarks, a metadata reconstruction challenge spanning six public datasets, and two end-to-end single-cell RNA-seq analyses using Allen Brain Atlas hippocampus and Tabula Sapiens large intestine datasets, alongside qualitative case studies demonstrating adaptive reasoning during analysis execution.\n                \n                  Abstract Figure\n                  \n                    \n                  \n                \n                \n                  IN BRIEF\n                  Modern single-cell and spatial omics studies generate datasets that are increasingly difficult to analyze manually, creating a growing bottleneck between data generation and biological discovery. CARIBOU is a multi-agent AI system designed to autonomously perform bioinformatics analyses within the high-performance computing environments used by research institutions. By combining specialized AI agents with persistent execution environments and built-in analytical workflows, CARIBOU can adaptively execute, troubleshoot, and document complex analyses while maintaining reproducibility and compatibility with real-world research infrastructure.","doi":"10.64898/2026.05.25.727730","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000337","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A multi-agent framework for bioinformatics and single-cell RNA-seq workflows, not a primary generative text-bio model. The abstract emphasizes execution, troubleshooting, and workflow orchestration rather than model training.","evidence_snippet":"\"multi-agent framework\" and \"iteratively execute, troubleshoot, and refine bioinformatics workflows\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000337","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a multi-agent framework for executing and refining bioinformatics workflows, not a generative text-bio model. It emphasizes orchestration, troubleshooting, and reproducible execution.","evidence_snippet":"\"multi-agent framework\" and \"iteratively execute, troubleshoot, and refine bioinformatics workflows\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000338","title":"The Emerging Role of Artificial Intelligence in Advancing Molecular Precision Medicine for Cancer","abstract":"and visualization but also in generative modeling tasks across a  Cell type annotation is a  time-consuming step in single-cell  GPT-4 enabled semi- or fully automated cell type annotation","doi":"10.1007/978-3-032-24071-2_13","year":"2026","venue":"Molecular Precision Medicine in Cancer","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000338","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This is a chapter/review on AI in cancer precision medicine, not a primary model paper. The abstract mentions GPT-4 and cell type annotation as examples rather than a new text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"The Emerging Role of Artificial Intelligence in Advancing Molecular Precision Medicine for Cancer\" and \"GPT-4 enabled semi- or fully automated cell type annotation\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000338","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This appears to be a review/chapter on AI in cancer precision medicine, not a primary model paper. The abstract only mentions GPT-4 and generative modeling as examples, not a new model.","evidence_snippet":"\"The Emerging Role of Artificial Intelligence...\" and \"GPT-4 enabled semi- or fully automated cell type annotation\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000339","title":"BioXArena: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Multi-Modal Biomedical Machine Learning Tasks","abstract":"for four closed-source models (GPT-5.4 [15], Claude Opus  small tables, whereas imaging,  single-cell, and structure tasks  models and structure-aware generative models such as ESM/","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000339","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title is explicitly benchmarking LLM agents on biomedical tasks, so this is a benchmark/resource paper. It evaluates models rather than proposing a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Benchmarking LLM Agents on Multi-Modal Biomedical Machine Learning Tasks\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000339","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract frame this as a benchmarking paper for LLM agents across biomedical tasks. It evaluates models rather than introducing a new generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"Benchmarking LLM Agents on Multi-Modal Biomedical Machine Learning Tasks\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]
