./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0224.stderr.log:1772:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0008.prompt.txt:37:- If the abstract only supports wrapper-style use of an existing LLM or language model, set `paper_type=application_wrapper` and `text_bio_bridge_present=no`.
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0224.stderr.log:1773:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0008.prompt.txt:52:[{"record_id":"rec_000149","title":"AI-Driven Early Diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder: Current Status and Future Perspectives","abstract":": Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a complex neurodevelopmental condition characterized by challenges in social communication, restricted interests, and repetitive behaviors. Early diagnosis and intervention are critical for improving long-term outcomes for individuals with ASD. In recent years, artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) techniques have emerged as powerful tools for assisting in the early detection and diagnosis of ASD. This review provides a comprehensive analysis of the current state of AI-driven approaches for ASD early diagnosis, examining applications across various data modalities including neuroimaging, eye-tracking, electroencephalography (EEG), clinical questionnaires, and genomic data. We systematically review recent advances in deep learning, traditional machine learning, and multimodal fusion techniques, evaluating their diagnostic performance and clinical applicability. Furthermore, we discuss current challenges including data heterogeneity, model interpretability, algorithmic fairness, and the need for large-scale validation studies. Finally, we outline future research directions and potential pathways for translating AI-based diagnostic tools into clinical practice. Our findings suggest that while significant progress has been made, continued interdisciplinary collaboration between computer scientists, clinicians, and researchers is essential for developing robust, interpretable, and clinically deployable AI systems for the early diagnosis of ASD.","doi":"10.25236/ajmhs.2026.070201","year":"2026","venue":"Academic Journal of Medicine &amp; Health Sciences","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000149","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review of AI-driven early ASD diagnosis across modalities, not a primary model paper. The abstract summarizes prior work and future directions rather than introducing a candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"“This review provides a comprehensive analysis of the current state of AI-driven approaches”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000149","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a review of AI-driven ASD diagnosis across modalities and methods. It does not describe a new generative architecture or provide foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"“This review provides a comprehensive analysis” and “deep learning, traditional machine learning, and multimodal fusion techniques”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000151","title":"Massively multiplex multimodal chemical screens at single-cell resolution","abstract":"Abstract\n                Recent applications of scRNA-seq for massively multiplexed chemical screens have enabled comprehensive profiling of drug responses at unprecedented scale and resolution. However, current assays remain limited to RNA readouts, lacking information on other phenotypic and mechanistic layers such as chromatin accessibility, protein abundance and post-translational modifications. Here, we introduce a scalable framework for multimodal chemical screens, combining parallel small-molecule perturbations with multimodal readouts. We extend existing experimental platforms into icCITE-plex and DOGMA-plex, enabling joint profiling of RNA, protein, and epigenomic responses to thousands of chemical perturbations in parallel. To systematically decode the regulatory circuitry underlying these responses, we develop MoCAVI, a contrastive analysis framework that disentangles the effect of small molecules from control variation in multimodal measurements, and PERCISTRA, a pipeline that infers causal links between chromatin accessibility and gene expression. Applied across ~410,000 primary T cells under ~2,800 conditions, our approach resolves compound-specific mechanisms, highlights off-target effects, and links chromatin accessibility changes to transcription factor networks in primary T cells. Our results establish a generalizable platform for profiling and analyzing cellular responses to chemical perturbations across multiple modalities.","doi":"10.64898/2026.05.23.727396","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000151","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a multimodal chemical-screening framework and analysis pipeline, not a text-bio foundational model. It is primarily a resource/methods paper.","evidence_snippet":"“introduce a scalable framework for multimodal chemical screens”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000151","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes multimodal chemical-screening analysis frameworks for decoding perturbation effects, not a generative model. It is a methods paper about screening and inference, with no foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"“we develop MoCAVI, a contrastive analysis framework” and “PERCISTRA, a pipeline that infers causal links”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000154","title":"Prototype Guided Post-pretraining for Single-Cell Representation Learning","abstract":"Single-cell representation learning (SCRL) from gene expression data offers a way to uncover the complex regulatory logic underlying cellular function. Inspired by large language models in natural language modeling, several single-cell pretrained models have recently been proposed that treat genes as tokens and cells as sentences. However, these models are fundamentally limited by the long-tailed nature of cell-type distributions and struggle to generalize under covariate shifts in gene expression data. While fine-tuning is often used to mitigate these issues, we observe that performance remains bounded. To address this challenge, we introduce CellRefine, a post-pretraining method that operates between the pretraining and fine-tuning stages of a single-cell foundation model. CellRefine uses a multi-faceted objective that incorporates marker-gene sets as structural priors to guide post-pretraining and refine the latent embedding manifold of cells. Across multiple computational biology tasks, empirical results show that CellRefine consistently improves downstream performance, yielding gains up to 15%.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["arxiv","google_scholar","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000154","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Presents a new post-pretraining method for single-cell representation learning on gene expression data. It is biology-only and does not include a text modality or text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“single-cell representation learning from gene expression data” and “genes as tokens and cells as sentences”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000154","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes post-pretraining representation refinement, not a generative architecture. It mentions a single-cell foundation model context, but the candidate method itself is not clearly foundation-model-like.","evidence_snippet":"“operates between the pretraining and fine-tuning stages of a single-cell foundation model”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000161","title":"DPRM: A Plug-in Doob h transform-induced Token-Ordering Module for Diffusion Language Models","abstract":"Diffusion language models generate without a fixed left-to-right order, making token ordering a central algorithmic choice: which tokens should be revealed, retained, revised or verified at each step? Existing systems mainly use random masking or confidence-driven ordering. Random masking creates train--test mismatch, while confidence-only rules are efficient but can be myopic and suppress useful exploration.   We introduce DPRM (Doob h-transform Process Reward Model), a plug-in token-ordering module for diffusion language models. DPRM keeps the host architecture, denoising objective and supervision unchanged, and changes only the ordering policy. It starts from confidence-driven progressive ordering and gradually shifts to Doob h transform Process Reward guided ordering through online estimates.   We characterize the exact DPRM policy as a reward-tilted Gibbs reveal law, prove O(1/N) convergence of the stagewise Soft-BoN approximation, and show that the online bucketized controller tracks the exact DPRM score at empirical-Bernstein rates. Under tractable optimization assumptions, DPRM also yields a sample-complexity advantage over random and confidence-only ordering.   DPRM improves over confidence-based baselines in pretraining, post-training, test-time scaling, and single-cell masked diffusion, with particularly strong gains on harder reasoning subsets. In protein, molecular generation and DNA design, the effect is more multi-objective: ordering-aware variants significantly improve selected structural or fragment-constrained metrics while not uniformly dominating the host baseline on every quality metric. These results identify token ordering as a fundamental control axis in diffusion language models and establish DPRM as a general-purpose module for improving it. Code is available at https://github.com/DakeBU/DPRM-DLLM.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["arxiv","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000161","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"A new token-ordering module is proposed for diffusion language models, with evaluations in protein, molecular generation, DNA design, and single-cell masked diffusion. The abstract does not clearly establish a substantive text-bio bridge beyond general language-model use and bio applications.","evidence_snippet":"“plug-in token-ordering module for diffusion language models” and “protein, molecular generation and DNA design”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000161","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A diffusion language model with a plug-in token-ordering module is explicitly generative. The abstract also describes broad use across pretraining, post-training, test-time scaling, and other domains.","evidence_snippet":"“Diffusion language models generate” and “improves over confidence-based baselines in pretraining, post-training, test-time scaling”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000164","title":"Pathway-Aware Multimodal Transformer for Explainable Survival Prediction in Bladder Cancer","abstract":"ABSTRACT – Cancer survival prediction requires effective integration of heterogeneous biomedical modalities such as gene expression profiles and histopathological whole-slide images (WSIs) to capture both molecular and morphological characteristics of tumors. Existing approaches often rely on unimodal learning or coarse multimodal fusion, limiting their ability to model fine-grained genotype-phenotype interactions. This paper presents a survey and system-oriented analysis of a pathway-aware multimodal framework for bladder urothelial carcinoma (BLCA) survival prediction based on the Pathway-Aware Multimodal Transformer (PAMT). The framework organizes gene expression data into biologically meaningful pathway representations while processing WSIs using patch-based feature extraction and transformer-based encoding. A contrastive alignment mechanism and pathway-to-patch cross-attention fusion module enable biologically guided multimodal interaction learning between genomic pathways and localized tissue regions. The integrated representation is utilized for survival risk estimation using a Cox proportional hazards-based objective, while attention-based interpretability mechanisms provide insights into prognostically significant pathways and tissue structures. The survey findings indicate that pathway-aware transformer architectures offer improved multimodal interaction modeling, enhanced interpretability, and strong potential for clinically explainable cancer survival prediction systems.\n\nKEY WORDS: Multimodal Learning, Survival Prediction, Bladder Cancer, Transformer Architecture, Explainable AI","doi":"10.55041/isjem07291","year":"2026","venue":"International Scientific Journal of Engineering and Management","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000164","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract states this is a “survey and system-oriented analysis,” not a primary model paper. It discusses gene expression and WSI-based survival prediction rather than any text-bio generative model.","evidence_snippet":"“This paper presents a survey and system-oriented analysis”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000164","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The abstract is framed as a “survey and system-oriented analysis,” even though it also describes a survival-prediction framework. The described model is predictive, not generative.","evidence_snippet":"“presents a survey and system-oriented analysis” and “survival risk estimation using a Cox proportional hazards-based objective”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000166","title":"AgriBioNER: A Named Entity Recognition Tool for Identification of\nncRNA and Diseases in Agricultural Literature","abstract":"The rapid growth of agricultural and genomic research has generated a vast\nbody of literature, making manual extraction of non-coding RNAs (ncRNAs) and agricultural disease entities increasingly challenging. Although pretrained transformer models such as BioBERTv1.1, PubMedBERT-base-uncased-abstract-fulltext, DeBERTa-v3-large, and RoBERTa-base perform well in biomedical NLP, they lack task-specific Named Entity Recognition (NER) layers\nadapted to agricultural terminology. This study presents AgriBioNER, a domain-adapted NER\nframework for accurately extracting ncRNA and disease entities from agricultural literature.\n \n \n \n To develop a robust NER system focused on disease-related ncRNAs, relevant scientific literature was systematically \nretrieved from PubMed. The collected abstracts underwent a comprehensive preprocessing phase in which non\ninformative components such as author names, institutional affiliations, and digital object identifiers (DOIs) were \nremoved to retain only the core textual content. Following this, manual annotation was performed to label key \nbiological entities of interest. The labeled data was then exported in two formats: a JSON format compatible with the \nspaCy NER pipeline and a BIO (Beginning-Inside-Outside) format [22], widely used in fine-tuning transformer-based \nmodels such as DeepSeek, BERT, and other LLMs. Based on this labeled corpus, a custom NER dataset was developed \nspecifically for the domain of ncRNA and disease associations. Using this dataset, seven different models were fine\ntuned to identify domain-specific entities. These models were then evaluated on a separate unseen test set, allowing \nfor an assessment of their generalization capability and real-world applicability (see Figure_1). \nDataset Preparation \nData Collection: \nWe collected abstracts related to ncRNAs and diseases within the agriculture domain, as in Figure_1 (raw data). We \nused an advanced query on PubMed, “Non-coding RNA AND Disease AND Agriculture NOT Human,” which \nreturned 2,652 (January 2025) total abstracts published between 1988 and 2025, as in Figure_2.\nData Preprocessing: \nThe raw data contains institute name, author name, DOI, journal name, etc., which are not required for our objective. \nSo, we have removed them manually from each abstract to make it more consistent and noiseless, as in Figure_1 \n(pre-processed data). \nData annotation: \nWe manually annotated and pre-processed 2,652 PubMed abstracts and carefully labeled ncRNAs, including their \ndifferent types as well as disease names. We labeled these entities as NON-CODING_RNA and DISEASE NER \nannotators for spaCy and Label Studio [23]. We exported the annotated data in two formats: JSON, which works well \nwith the spaCy [24] pipeline, and the BIO CoNLL-2003 [22] format, which is commonly used in other biomedical \nNLP. The JSON schema shows the text and its entities along with their positions within the text, as in Figure_1 (JSON \nformat). The BIO tagging scheme consists of three labels, i.e., Beginning (B), Inside (I), and Outside (O). The B \n(Beginning) tag is used to mark the first word of an entity, indicating both the start of the entity and its type. If an \nentity spans multiple words, only the first word is tagged with B, while the subsequent words are tagged with I (Inside) \nto show that they are part of the same entity but not at its beginning. Tokens that do not belong to any entity are labeled \nwith O (Outside), as in Figure_1 (BIO format). \nAfter labeling, the custom dataset (D) was created, as in equation (1). \n�\n�\n \n \n \n A curated dataset of agricultural abstracts related to ncRNAs and diseases was retrieved\nfrom PubMed. Four transformer models were fine-tuned using the spaCy transformer pipeline, and\na classical spaCy model (en_core_web_lg) was trained separately. Performance was evaluated using precision, recall, F1-score, statistical significance testing, 500-iteration bootstrap validation, error analysis, and assessment of prediction time and scalability\n \n \n \n Transformer models showed consistent performance, while en_core_web_lg exhibited\nhigher asymmetric errors. PubMedBERT-base-uncased-abstract-fulltext achieved the best results\nwith a precision of 0.8351 ± 0.0139, recall of 0.8732 ± 0.0117, and F1-score of 0.8537 ± 0.0111. It\nprocessed abstracts in approximately 3.56 seconds per abstract, supporting large-scale deployment.\n \n \n \n Domain-adapted transformer models substantially improved ncRNA–disease entity\nrecognition in agricultural texts. Enhanced contextual representations enabled a better understanding of domain-specific terminology. These improvements demonstrate the effectiveness of specialized fine-tuning for agricultural NLP tasks.\n \n \n \n AgriBioNER offers a reliable framework for automated extraction of ncRNA and disease entities. The system reduces manual curation efforts while maintaining high accuracy and\ncomputational efficiency. It supports data-driven advancements in agricultural genomics and biotechnology research.","doi":"10.2174/0113892029435693260407094051","year":"2026","venue":"Current Genomics","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000166","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"no","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC1_no_bio_modality","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a text-only NER system trained on agricultural abstracts to extract ncRNA and disease entities. The abstract does not present a biological data modality as the model input or target modality.","evidence_snippet":"“Named Entity Recognition Tool for Identification of ncRNA and Diseases in Agricultural Literature”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000166","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"AgriBioNER is a domain-adapted NER framework built by fine-tuning pretrained transformers for a specific extraction task. That is task-specific application wrapping, not a generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“domain-adapted NER framework” and “seven different models were fine-tuned”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC1_no_bio_modality","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"no","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
